edb/ref 17592186168915}] :question/id #uuid "b3cf0cec-6551-5e6d-9467-114834906da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159281" :question/problem "Consider two independent samples of sizes $n_{x} = 16$ and $n_{y} = 20$ from normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with standard deviations $σ_{x} = 2$ and $σ_{y} = 3$, respectively. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $μ_{x}$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $μ_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nGiven that the sample means are $x^(―) = 22$ and $y^(―) = 20$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $5%$ **one-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\ge 1.645$\n2. At the $5%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $μ_{x} > μ_{y}$\n3. At the $5%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $μ_{x} > μ_{y}$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67425653-1c20-5713-b2cb-5569c26c2cf9" :knowledge-point/title "Testing a Hypothesis Given a Normal Population: One-Tailed Tests" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050729 :topic/math-academy-id 3303 :topic/title "Hypothesis Tests for One Mean: Known Population Variance"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108321e6-218b-5001-bc9a-ed191eb5cd2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 175921