ledge-point/id #uuid "f49d259b-3b28-5d23-ae93-d1c251c0b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Testing a Hypothesis for the Mean: Applications" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048509 :topic/math-academy-id 3313 :topic/title "Hypothesis Tests for Two Means: Equal But Unknown Population Variances"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2df259-544b-59fd-a823-96353b87f1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170487} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6f88cc-35a6-5b04-a45c-8debd32ee858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170488} {:answer/id #uuid "01425c4e-e176-50ca-bd85-aae1b3470b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170489} {:answer/id #uuid "07308ffb-17e8-572d-b9f7-d83b9b800ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170490} {:answer/id #uuid "197cc49b-97b7-5444-8cfe-1b514616e7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170491}] :answer-field/id #uuid "413ec2d7-3cbc-59cf-96ca-80b2f2170ed0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170486}] :question/id #uuid "6ca8a644-1213-5bd7-bcf8-7e3f1f956920" :question/math-academy-id "q-251630" :question/problem "The battery lives of two phone models, X and Y, are known to be normally distributed with equal population variances. Statisticians want to determine whether the population mean $μ_{x}$ of the battery life of model X is different from the population mean $μ_{y}$ of the battery life of model Y. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10%$ significance level.\n\nThe statisticians sampled $28$ phones of model X and $25$ phones of model Y and found that the first sample has a mean battery life of $10$ hours and a standard deviation of $1.5$ hours, while the second sample has a mean battery life of $9$ hours and a standard deviation of $2$ hours.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $10%$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $-1.675 \\le T \\le 1.675$\n2. At the $10%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $μ_{x} \\ne μ_{y}$\n3. At the $10%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $μ_{x} \\ne μ_{y}$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $50$ | $1.299$ | $1.676$ | $2.009$ | $2.403$ | $2.678$ |\n| $51$ | $1.298$ | $1.675$ | $2.008$ | $2.402$ | $2.676$ |\n| $52$ | $1.298$ | $1.675$ | $2.007$ | $2.400$ | $2.674$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
